    content: `<p><strong>Vector Algebra</strong> deals with vectors, which have both magnitude and direction, unlike scalars that only have magnitude. The main operations include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Vector addition and subtraction.</li>
<li>Scalar multiplication.</li>
<li>Dot product.</li>
<li>Cross product.</li>
<li>Resolution of vectors.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Addition and Subtraction</strong></h2>
<p>Vectors are added to combine multiple quantities into a single resultant vector using various methods:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Head-to-Tail Method</strong>: Place the tail of one vector at the head of another to form a resultant vector from the first tail to the final head.</li>
<li><strong>Parallelogram Method</strong>: Place two vectors to form adjacent sides of a parallelogram. The diagonal of the parallelogram represents the resultant vector.</li>
<li><strong>Triangle Method</strong>: Similar to the head-to-tail method but specifically involves reversing one vector if needed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example 1: Head-to-Tail Method</strong></p>
<p>Consider two vectors A and B represented as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li>A = 3 m (east).</li>
<li>B = 4 m (north).</li>
</ul>
<p>Find the resultant vector R when adding A and B, use the head-to-tail method:</p>
<p>Place the tail of vector B at the head of vector A. Draw the resultant vector R from the tail of A to the head of B.</p>

<p>The magnitude of the resultant vector R can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\text{Magnitude of} R=\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}\\)</span></p>
<p>Substitute values:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}=\\sqrt{9 + 16}=\\sqrt{25} = 5 \\, \\text{m}\\)</span></p>
<p>The direction of the resultant vector R can be found using trigonometry:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\theta=\\tan^{-1} \\left(\\frac{B}{A}\\right)\\)</span></p>
<p>Substitute values:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\theta=\\tan^{-1} \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) = 53.13^\\circ\\)</span></p>
<p>So, the resultant vector R is approximately 5 m at an angle of 53.13<sup>∘</sup> north of east.</p>
<p><strong>Example 2: Parallelogram Method</strong></p>
<p>Find the resultant of two vectors of 3 units and 4 units acting at a point O at an angle of 60<sup>∘</sup> to each other.</p>
<p>To find the resultant vector R when adding C and D, use the parallelogram method:</p>
<p>Draw vector C as the first side of the parallelogram. Draw vector D as the second side of the parallelogram, with its tail connected to the head of vector C. Complete the parallelogram by drawing the diagonal, which represents the resultant vector R.</p>

<p>The magnitude of the resultant vector R can be calculated using the cosine rule:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\text{Magnitude of} R=\\sqrt{C^2 + D^2 - 2(C)(D)\\cos(\\text{angle})}\\)</span></p>
<p>Substitute values:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2 - 2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\times \\cos 120^\\circ}\\)</span></p>
<p>The direction of the resultant vector R can be found using the sine rule:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\alpha=\\sin^{-1} \\left(\\frac{3 \\times \\sin 120}{6.78}\\right)\\)</span></p>
<p>Calculate:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\alpha = 22.53^\\circ\\)</span></p>
<p>So, the resultant vector R is approximately 5.83 m at an angle of 22.53<sup>∘</sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Example 3: Triangle Method</strong></p>
<p>Two forces 5 N and 4 N are inclined to each other at 30<sup>∘</sup>. Find the resultant force by the triangle method.</p>
<p>To find the resultant force, draw OA=5 cm to represent the 5 N force using a scale of 1 cm=1 N. From the tip A, draw AB = 4 cm inclined to OA at 30<sup>∘</sup>. Join OB, which represents the resultant vector in magnitude and direction.</p>

<p><strong>Question 1: Vector Addition</strong></p>
<p>If vector A has components (3, 4) and vector B has components (-2, 1), find the magnitude of the resultant vector A + B.</p>
<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>
<p>Vector A = (3, 4), Vector B = (-2, 1)</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>To find the resultant vector A + B, we add the corresponding components:
	<ul>
<li>A + B = (3 + (-2), 4 + 1) = (1, 5).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>The magnitude of a vector (x, y) is given by √(x² + y²).</li>
<li>Magnitude of A + B = √(1² + 5²) = √26 units.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Question 2: Vector Subtraction</strong></p>
<p>Given vector P = 5i + 12j and vector Q = 4i - 3j, determine the direction of vector R = P - Q.</p>
<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>
<p>Vector P = 5i + 12j, Vector Q = 4i - 3j</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>R = P - Q = (5i + 12j) - (4i - 3j) = i + 15j.</li>
<li>Direction of a vector can be found using the formula: θ = tan⁻¹(y/x).</li>
<li>θ = tan⁻¹(15/1) ≈ 86.41° (measured from the positive x-axis).</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Multiplication</strong></h2>
<p>Multiplying a vector by a scalar changes its magnitude but not its direction.</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(k\\vec{v}=(kv_x, kv_y, kv_z)\\)</span></p>
<p>Question 3: Scalar Multiplication</p>
<p>A vector U has a magnitude of 10 units and points north. If it is multiplied by a scalar of -0.5, what is the magnitude and direction of the resulting vector?</p>
<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>
<p>Vector U has a magnitude of 10 units pointing north, scalar = -0.5</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Multiplying a vector by a negative scalar reverses its direction.</li>
<li>New magnitude = 0.5 * 10 = 5 units.</li>
<li>New direction: South (opposite of north).</li>
</ul>

<h2><strong>Dot Product (Scalar Product)</strong></h2>
<p>The dot product of two vectors is a scalar value that measures the cosine of the angle between them, indicative of their directional alignment.</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\vec{u} \\cdot \\vec{v} = u_xv_x + u_yv_y + u_zv_z\\)</span></p>
<p>Find the dot product of</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\vec{P}=(1, 3, -5) \\text{and} \\vec{Q}=(7, -6, -2)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\vec{P} \\cdot \\vec{Q} = 1 \\cdot 7 + 3 \\cdot (-6) + (-5) \\cdot (-2)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 7 - 18 + 10 = -1\\)</span></p>
<p><strong>Question 4: Dot Product</strong></p>
<p>Find the dot product of vectors A = 2i - j + 3k and B = 4i + 2j - k.</p>
<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>
<p>Vector A = 2i - j + 3k, Vector B = 4i + 2j - k</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The dot product of two vectors A = a₁i + a₂j + a₃k and B = b₁i + b₂j + b₃k is given by: A • B = a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃.</li>
<li>A • B = (2)(4) + (-1)(2) + (3)(-1) = 8 - 2 - 3 = 3.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Cross Product (Vector Product)</strong></h2>
<p>The cross product of two vectors results in a third vector perpendicular to both, significant in physics for determining rotational effects.</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\vec{u} \\times \\vec{v}=(u_yv_z - u_zv_y, u_zv_x - u_xv_z, u_xv_y - u_yv_x)\\)</span></p>
<p>Vector A = 2i + 3j - k and Vector B = i - j + 2k. Find the cross product A x B.</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<p>The cross product of two vectors A = a₁i + a₂j + a₃k and B = b₁i + b₂j + b₃k is given by:</p>
<p>A x B = |i j k|</p>
<p>|a₁ a₂ a₃|</p>
<p>|b₁ b₂ b₃|</p>
<p>Expanding the determinant, we get:</p>
<p>A x B = i(a₂b₃ - a₃b₂) - j(a₁b₃ - a₃b₁) + k(a₁b₂ - a₂b₁)</p>
<p>Substituting the given values:</p>
<p>A x B = i((3)(2) - (-1)(-1)) - j((2)(2) - (-1)(1)) + k((2)(-1) - (3)(1))</p>
<p>= i(6 - 1) - j(4 + 1) + k(-2 - 3)</p>
<p>= 5i - 5j - 5k</p>
<p>Therefore, the cross product A x B is 5i - 5j - 5k.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Resolution Of Vectors</strong></h1>
<p>When a vector is broken down into its components, it is referred to as the resolution of the vector. This is commonly done for vectors that do not align with the axes (e.g., not parallel to the x or y axis).</p>
<p>Vectors can be in 2D or 3D. A 2D vector exists in a two-dimensional plane, typically defined by the x and y axes. It can be represented as an ordered pair (x, y).</p>
<p>A 3D vector exists in a three-dimensional space, typically defined by the x, y, and z axes. It can be represented as an ordered triplet (x, y, z).</p>
<p>Vectors can be resolved using analytic or graphical method. To graphically resolve a vector, you can use a scale diagram.</p>

<p><strong>Graphical Method of Solution</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Draw the Vector</strong>: Draw the vector as an arrow with the appropriate length and direction.</li>
<li><strong>Draw Parallel Lines</strong>: Draw lines that are parallel to the coordinate axes (usually horizontal and vertical) to create a rectangle with the vector as one side.</li>
<li><strong>Measure Lengths</strong>: Measure the lengths of the sides of the rectangle.</li>
<li><strong>Component Representation</strong>: These lengths represent the components of the vector in the respective directions.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Analytical Method</strong></h2>
<p>Horizontal (x) Component (Vx):</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(V_x = V \\cos(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p>Vertical (y) Component (Vy):</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(V_y = V \\sin(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p><strong>Resultant Vector (Component Method)</strong></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(R=\\sqrt{(\\Sigma F_x)^2 + (\\Sigma F_y)^2}\\)</span></p>
<p><strong>Direction (Angle of Resultant)</strong></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\theta=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\Sigma F_y}{\\Sigma F_x}\\right)\\)</span></p>
<p>Relationship between force and resultant</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\cos \\theta=\\frac{F}{R}\\)</span></p>
<h2><strong>Question 1</strong></h2>
<p>A force of 200 N is applied at an angle of 30 degrees to the horizontal. Find the horizontal and vertical components of this force.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<p>Given:</p>
<ul>
<li>Force (F) = 200 N.</li>
<li>Angle (θ) = 30 degrees.</li>
</ul>
<p>Horizontal Component:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\((F_x) = F \\cos(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(F_x = 200 \\text{N} \\times \\cos(30^\\circ)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 200 \\text{N} \\times 0.866\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 173.2 \\text{N}\\)</span></p>
<p>Vertical Component:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\((F_y) = F \\sin(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(F_y = 200 \\text{N} \\times \\sin(30^\\circ)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 200 \\text{N} \\times 0.5\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 100 \\text{N}\\)</span></p>
<h2><strong>Question 2</strong></h2>
<p>An object moves at a velocity of 15 m/s at an angle of 60 degrees to the horizontal. Find the horizontal and vertical components of this velocity.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
<p>Given:</p>
<ul>
<li>Velocity (V) = 15 m/s.</li>
<li>Angle (θ) = 60 degrees.</li>
</ul>
<p>Horizontal Component:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\((V_x) = V \\cos(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(V_x = 15 \\text{m/s} \\times \\cos(60^\\circ)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 15 \\text{m/s} \\times 0.5\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 7.5 \\text{m/s}\\)</span></p>
<p>Vertical Component:</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\((V_y) = V \\sin(\\theta)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(V_y = 15 \\text{m/s} \\times \\sin(60^\\circ)\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(= 15 \\text{m/s} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)</span></p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(\\approx 12.99 \\text{m/s}\\)</span></p>
<h2><strong>Question 3</strong></h2>
<p>Two force each of 10N act on a body, one towards the north and the other towards the east. The magnitude and the direction of the resultant force are what?</p>
<p>Solution</p>

<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(R=\\sqrt {10^2+10^2}=10\\sqrt2\\)</span></p>
<p>θ = tan<sup>-1</sup> (<sup>10</sup>⁄10)</p>
<p>= tan<sup>-1</sup>(1) = 45<sup>0</sup>E</p>
<h2><strong>Question 4</strong></h2>
<p>A hand bag containing some load weighing 162N is carried by two students each holding the handle of the bag next to him. If each handle is pulled 60<sup>0</sup> to the vertical, find the force on each student arm.</p>

<p>TCos60<sup>0</sup></p>
<p>TCos60<sup>0</sup></p>
<p>(TCos60<sup>0</sup> + TCos60<sup>0</sup>) – 162 = 0</p>
<p>TCos60<sup>0</sup> + TCos60<sup>0</sup>= 162</p>
<p>T (<sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>2</sub> ) + T (<sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>2</sub> ) = 162</p>
<p>T = 162 N</p>
<h2><strong>Question 5</strong></h2>
<p>If the angle between two vectors P and Q is 0<sup>0</sup>, the vectors are said to be parallel</p>
<p><strong>Example</strong></p>

<p>The figure above shows 4 forces 3N, 10N, 3√3N and 6N acting on a particle P. The resultant of the four forces is?</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<p>For the horizontal components, F<sub>x</sub></p>
<p>3Ncos0 = 3N</p>
<p>10cos 30<sup>o</sup> = 8.7N</p>
<p>3√3 cos 90<sup>o</sup> = 0N</p>
<p>-6cos60 = -3N</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(H = F_{2x} + F_{3x} - F_{4x} = 8.66 + 3 - 3 = 8.66\\)</span></p>
<p>Sum of the horizontal components, Σf<sub>x</sub> = 8.7N</p>
<p>For the vertical components, F<sub>y</sub></p>
<p>3Nsin0 = 0</p>
<p>10sin30 = 5N</p>
<p>3√3 sin90 = 5.2N</p>
<p>-6sin60<sup>o</sup>= -5.2N</p>
<p><span class="mathjax-latex">\\(V = F_{1y} + F_{2y} - F_{4y} = 5.20 + 5 - 5.20 = 5\\)</span></p>
<p>Sum of the vertical components, Σf<sub>y</sub> = 5N</p>
<p>R = √(Σf<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup> + Σf<sub>y</sub><sup>2</sup>)</p>
<p>R = (5<sup>2</sup>+8.7<sup>2</sup>)</p>
<p>The resultant force is 10N.</p>
<h2><strong>Question 6</strong></h2>

<p>The diagram above shows forces 4N, 6N, 10N, and 8N which act at a point O in the directions indicated. The net horizontal force is ?</p>
<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<p>H = (10N + 8Cos60<sup>0</sup> + 6Cos60<sup>0</sup>) – 4</p>
<p>= 10 +8(<sup>1</sup>⁄2) + 6 (<sup>1</sup>⁄2) – 4</p>
<p>H = 10 +4 + 3 – 4</p>
<p>13N</p>
<h2><strong>Question 7</strong></h2>
<p>The net vertical force in the diagram below is?</p>

<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<p>(25N + 20Sin60<sup>0</sup> + 30Cos45) – 20N)</p>
<p>25 + 20 (<sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>2</sub> ) + 30 (<sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>2</sub> )) – 20</p>
<p>H = 25 + 10 + 15 – 20</p>
<p>H = 30</p>`
