    content: `<p>Physics supplies principles that explain how living systems work and underpins much medical and biological technology.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Application Areas</strong></h1>
<ol>
<li><strong>Biomechanics</strong> — applies mechanics to organisms; explains muscle contraction and joint movement, locomotion (walking, running), and sports performance/injury prevention.</li>
<li><strong>Fluid dynamics</strong> — describes fluid movement in the body, especially blood flow. Bernoulli's principle aids understanding of vessel flow and diagnosis/treatment of cardiovascular conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Electrophysiology</strong> — studies electrical properties of tissues: nerve impulses, cardiac rhythms, and brain activity.</li>
<li><strong>Medical imaging</strong> — X-ray, MRI, CT, and ultrasound create internal images from how X-rays, magnetic fields, or sound waves interact with tissue.</li>
<li><strong>Radiation therapy</strong> — delivers radiation to destroy cancer cells while sparing healthy tissue; relies on accurate dose calculation, planning, and delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong> — how drugs distribute, metabolize, and are eliminated; diffusion and transport concepts guide formulation and dosing.</li>
<li><strong>Molecular biophysics</strong> — NMR spectroscopy and X-ray crystallography reveal 3D structures of proteins, DNA, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Genomics/DNA sequencing</strong> — next-generation sequencing detects electrical or optical signals tied to DNA bases.</li>
<li><strong>Acoustics and hearing</strong> — explains sound production/propagation and ear mechanics; supports audiology and hearing-aid design.</li>
<li><strong>Thermodynamics</strong> — describes metabolism, energy transfer, heat generation, and temperature regulation.</li>
<li><strong>Biophotonics</strong> — uses light-based technologies for imaging, microscopy, and sensing.</li>
<li><strong>Nanotechnology</strong> — nanoscale devices for drug delivery, imaging, and cellular/molecular diagnostics.</li>
</ol>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Ultrasound</strong></h1>
<p>Ultrasound images internal structures using high-frequency sound waves (2–15 MHz).</p>
<p><strong>Piezoelectric effect (probe function):</strong> certain crystals vibrate at a set frequency when a voltage is applied, converting electrical energy to kinetic energy and producing ultrasound waves. The same crystals also generate a voltage when mechanically vibrated, letting the probe detect reflected waves.</p>
<h2>Imaging Principles</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spatial resolution</strong> — ability to distinguish two objects as distinct. <em>Axial:</em> minimum separation along the beam (one above the other); <em>Lateral:</em> minimum separation side-by-side (perpendicular to the beam).</li>
<li><strong>Contrast resolution</strong> — ability to distinguish two objects of similar echogenicity.</li>
<li><strong>Frequency vs. penetration</strong> — higher frequency gives better resolution but less penetration; lower frequency gives poorer resolution but greater penetration, so it images deep structures.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Wave Interactions with Tissue</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Absorbed</strong> — sound energy becomes heat; absorption rises as tissue water content falls.</li>
<li><strong>Reflected</strong> — wave returns to the probe off a boundary. Depends on the difference in sound conduction between tissues, angle of incidence (best near 90°), and surface smoothness.
	<ul>
<li><em>Echogenicity</em> depends on returning amplitude: larger amplitude → whiter image.</li>
<li><em>Depth</em> depends on echo return time (proportional to twice the object's distance): <strong>d = vt/2</strong>, where <em>d</em> = depth, <em>v</em> = speed of sound in tissue (≈1540 m s⁻¹), <em>t</em> = time.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Transmitted</strong> — sound continues deeper, where it may reflect or be absorbed.</li>
<li><strong>Scattered</strong> — sound reflects in many directions and may not return to the probe.</li>
<li><strong>Attenuated</strong> — overall reduction in wave strength with depth from absorption, reflection, scattering, and transmission. Managed by <em>gain</em> (amplifies returned signal) and <em>time-gain compensation</em> (boosts later-returning signals to reveal deep tissue).</li>
</ol>
<h2>Modes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>B-mode (brightness)</strong> — standard 2D display; plots reflected-wave amplitude against calculated depth.</li>
<li><strong>M-mode (movement)</strong> — tracks one vertical line over time (depth on y-axis, time on x-axis); useful for observing motion.</li>
</ul>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) Spectroscopy</strong></h1>
<p>NMR uses the magnetic properties of certain nuclei to determine physical and chemical properties of atoms or molecules, giving information on structure, dynamics, reaction state, and chemical environment.</p>
<p><strong>History:</strong> the unusual behavior of nuclei in strong magnetic fields (linked to the Zeeman effect) was observed by Zeeman in the late 19th century. NMR was first detected experimentally in 1945 by the groups of Felix Bloch and Edward Purcell; the first spectrum was published in January 1946, and the two shared the 1952 Nobel Prize in Physics.</p>
<p><strong>Use in organic chemistry:</strong> determines molecular structure, assesses content and purity, and distinguishes environments of nuclei such as ¹H and ¹³C.</p>
<h2>Principle</h2>
<p>All nuclei are charged and many have spin. An external magnetic field lets energy transfer from a lower to a higher energy state at radio frequency (RF); when nuclei return to the base level, energy is emitted at the same frequency. Measuring this signal produces the NMR spectrum.</p>
<h2>How It Works</h2>
<p>Place the sample in a strong magnetic field, apply radio waves to excite nuclei into resonance, and detect the resulting signals with sensitive receivers. Because resonance frequency is influenced by intramolecular magnetic fields, the data reveal functional groups and electronic structure, and thus reaction state, structure, chemical environment, and dynamics.</p>
<h2>Chemical Shift</h2>
<p>A spinning charge produces a magnetic moment proportional to spin. In an external field, nuclei occupy two spin states (aligned with or opposed to the field). <strong>Chemical shift</strong> is the difference between the resonant frequency of spinning protons and that of a reference molecule, and is central to determining structure. Commonly detected nuclei: ¹H, ¹³C, ¹⁵N, ¹⁹F.</p>
<h2>Instrumentation</h2>
<p>Sample holder (glass tube, 8.5 cm × 0.3 cm); magnetic coils (produce field when current flows); permanent magnet (homogeneous field, 60–100 MHz); sweep generator (adjusts field strength); RF transmitter (short RF pulses); RF receiver system (detects signals); RF detector (identifies unabsorbed RF); recorder; and computer readout/storage system.</p>
<h2>Techniques &amp; Applications</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Resonant frequency</strong> — NMR-active nuclei absorb radiation at a frequency characteristic of the isotope; signal intensity depends on field strength.</li>
<li><strong>Spectrum acquisition</strong> — after an RF pulse, the weak NMR response requires sensitive receivers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Applications: studying organic molecules (or any spin-containing sample), quantitative analysis of known mixtures, matching spectra to libraries or inferring unknown structures, and studying conformation in solution plus properties such as conformational exchange, phase changes, solubility, and diffusion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>Note from original: see Chapter 3 for X-ray notes.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Power Generation</strong></h1>
<p>Power generation produces electricity using technologies old (steam-based) and new (wind turbines). Most systems generate electricity by spinning a device inside an <strong>alternator</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Alternator principle:</strong> motors convert electricity into motion; alternators do the reverse. A <strong>rotor</strong> spins and produces a rotating magnetic field, which induces current in the <strong>stator</strong> (stationary copper windings around an iron core). This current feeds the power grid.</p>
<p><strong>Power generator:</strong> a self-contained source built around a reciprocating engine and an alternator. Sizes range from small portable units to very large ones, running on diesel, gasoline, biofuels, or natural gas. Uses: primary power where no reliable grid exists (e.g., some military bases) and standby power where outages are unacceptable (e.g., hospitals, data centers).</p>
<h2 style="text-align:center">Types of Power Plants</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Hydroelectric &amp; wind</strong> — generate without fuel using moving fluids (water flow; air movement) to spin turbine blades on a shaft connected to an alternator. Dam-based hydro harnesses water's potential energy.</li>
<li><strong>Solar</strong> —
	<ul>
<li><em>Photovoltaic:</em> sunlight photons strike silicon cells and release electrons; cell design drives electrons one way, creating current through a load.</li>
<li><em>Concentrated solar:</em> mirrors focus sunlight on a receiver that heats a boiler, producing steam to drive a turbine and alternator.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Geothermal</strong> — underground pipes carry water heated by geothermal heat; the resulting steam drives a turbine and alternator (e.g., Yellowstone geysers).</li>
<li><strong>Gas-fired (gas turbine)</strong> — air is compressed, mixed with fuel in a combustor and ignited; hot pressurized gases spin turbine blades whose shaft drives an alternator. Jet engines work similarly, but the torque spins fan blades for propulsion instead of driving an alternator.</li>
</ol>`
